Asthma.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Emily Hernandez is a 20-year-old female who presents for her yearly follow-up. Due to insurance changes since her last office appointment in 04/2020, she was switched from Symbicort to Wixela. She uses Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall for her seasonal allergy symptoms, which tend to work well. She also has known peanut allergy, she does tolerate almonds and cashews.

The patient reports she has been trying to reduce her daily inhaler use. She states that she has been using her albuterol inhaler about 3 to 4 days per week. She stated that she does not need it on days she is not exercising. She stated that she is trying to get into the military and that she is trying to prove that she can operate without the use of an inhaler. She stated that she has been using her Wixela about 3 to 4 days per week and that she takes it either in the morning or at night. She stated that she has not had to use her albuterol inhaler in the past year. She denied any urgent care or ER visits for breathing in the past year. She has not been on prednisone for breathing issues. She stated that she has been trying to use her daily inhaler less as she is trying to get into the military soon.

The patient has been using Zyrtec as needed in the summer. She stated that she does not need it in the summer. She stated that she uses it on days she knows she will be outside a lot. She stated that she works outside 3 days a week and she will definitely take it on those days. She stated that it does work well for her.

The patient reported that she got into the habit of vaping and smoking, but she has been trying to cut that out. She stated that she has been smoking a little bit and she is trying to quit. She stated that she is sure that this may have an effect.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Respiratory: Endorses mild asthma.
• Allergy: Endorses allergies to peanuts. Denies allergies to tree nuts except for almonds and cashews.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Auscultation of Lungs: Bilateral breath sounds are clear and equal bilaterally. No wheezing or rales noted.

RESULTS

The results of the pulmonary function testing reveal that her FEV1 is 98.5% predicted, FVC is 100% predicted, and FEV1/FVC is 85%. The act score is 21.

ASSESSMENT

Asthma.
Allergies.

PLAN

Asthma.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her asthma. She has been using her inhaler as prescribed and has not needed to use her albuterol inhaler. Her pulmonary function testing reveals that she is doing well. She is trying to reduce her daily inhaler use and get into the military.
• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of continuing to use her inhaler as needed. I advised her that she can continue to use her Wixela as she has been using it. I also advised her that she may need to use her albuterol inhaler as needed. I explained that she may need to use her albuterol inhaler during her military fitness test. I advised her that she may need to switch to a different inhaler if her symptoms worsen.
• Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Alvesco at 80 mcg, 2 puffs twice a day. I will call in a new Epipen for the patient. I will also renew her albuterol inhaler.

Allergies.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well with her allergies. She has been using her Zyrtec as needed.
• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed her allergies and the importance of continuing to use her Zyrtec as needed.
• Medical Treatment: She will continue to use Zyrtec as needed.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

