summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is a 86-year-old male who presents in follow up for adult hydrocephalus. The patient was last seen in office 3 months after undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery. The patient reports that his walking has improved, he is able to walk to the dining room without assistance. The patient reports that his bladder control improved after surgery but has leveled off and he is currently wearing a diaper. The patient denies any pain or discomfort from the shunt or any issues with sores from the diaper. The patient reports that he is doing much better than he was before his surgery. The patient is accompanied by his daughter.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient has a history of adult hydrocephalus.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient is retired.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking his medications as directed.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fever, unintentional weight loss.
Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.
Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies genital sores, decreased or painful output.
Neurological: Reports walking impairment, cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 124/80.
Heart rate: Regular rate and rhythm at 64.
Respiratory rate: 18.
Weight: 174 pounds.
Pain: 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neuro: Pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily. Daughter adds details. Shunt site is clean, dry, and intact with confirmed setting of 2.
Cognitive: Slight improvement in attention span and concentration. Scores 26/30 on the mini mental status exam when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. He was able to get 2 of 3 memory words with cuing, and the third with multiple choice. This is a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling. At the time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice.
Motor: Cranial nerves grossly intact. Pupils equal. EOMs intact. Face symmetric. No dysarchria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no- finger to nose. Of note, the CT scan from the head of 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of monro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 millimeters. By my reading there is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

RESULTS

CT scan of the head 10/15/2020 is reviewed and demonstrates a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of monro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 millimeters. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus
Gait impairment
Urinary incontinence and urgency
Cognitive impairment

PLAN

I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient in detail. The patient will follow up in 3 months. I advised the patient that it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level that would be needed for him to pass a driver safety evaluation. However, I also advised him that he could potentially improve over a 9 to 12 month range and that we could continue to watch for improvement. I also advised him that he could enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation and continue to watch for improvement. The patient is currently not driving and is using public transportation or relying on his daughter for transportation. The patient is happy with this arrangement and is not desirous of driving at this time. He is satisfied with the current treatment plan. He will continue to wear the diaper for now.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
