summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain, swelling, and edema.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a 71-year-old male who presents for an orthopedic follow up today. He was seen last in the office 2 months ago for his rheumatoid arthritis. At that time he received a steroid injection in his right knee. He states the injection provided him with relief for a couple of weeks. He notes that his knee is still swollen and painful. He rates his pain level as 6 out of 10. He has been wearing compression socks, but they have not been helpful. He states he has slowed down and has found ways to avoid activities that make his pain worse. He notes that he is stiffer when getting up from sitting. He is using a cane for support. He has been taking Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily.

Mr. Nelson reports that his right knee swelling has been present for 2 months. The swelling has extended to his entire leg. The swelling is not as bad as it was before he started wearing compression socks. He denies any numbness or tingling in his toes. He reports that the right knee is hot to the touch. He denies any redness. He states he can feel a firm mass in his right knee.

MEDICATIONS

Xeljanz 11 mg daily.
Celebrex 200 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain, swelling, and edema. Endocrine: Denies numbness or tingling in the toes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee: Range of motion is slightly diminished. There is pitting edema around the right knee and ankle. Normal capillary refill in the toes. The knee is warm to the touch.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee reveals a degenerative joint disease.

ASSESSMENT

Right knee pain, swelling, and edema.
Rheumatoid arthritis.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an x-ray of the right knee to further evaluate the cause of his swelling. I have also recommended that we refer him to physical therapy. If conservative measures do not improve his symptoms, we may need to consult with an orthopedic surgeon. I have also advised him to reduce his salt intake and elevate his legs when he is sitting or lying down. I have recommended that he follow up with his primary care physician to check his CMP levels. I have also recommended that he continue taking Celebrex 200 mg daily. I have recommended that we perform a steroid injection to the right knee.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care physician. He will follow up with me in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>
