Dermatologic symptoms.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a 50-year-old female who presents for follow-up today. The patient was last seen in the clinic approximately 1 week ago. She states that she is doing well. The patient reports that she has some leftover rash. She started using doxycycline once a day as she thinks the pharmacist told her to use it only once a day if it is doing better. She notes that she took it twice a day for the first 2 weeks and she did notice a lot of improvement, and then she started doing it only once a day and she notes that it seems the same and it is not continuing to get better. She is not currently breastfeeding.

The patient states that she stopped breastfeeding and then asked for doxycycline at that time. The patient is unsure if she has any other medications. She denies using any other lotions or creams. She is currently using sulfacetamide wash twice a day, metro-nidazole cream after washing her face, and she is also using Cetaphil cleanser. She denies using Neutrogena wipes. She is not using any lotion at this time.

The patient is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On physical exam, there are a few erythematous papules on the bilateral medial cheeks, as well as a little bit of erythema around and underneath her nares.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Rosacea.
Ocular rosacea.

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

Doxycycline, 100 mg twice a day.
Sulfacetamide wash.
Metro-nidazole cream.

ALLERGIES

None known.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Eyes: Conjunctiva is pink and without lesions.
Ears, Nose, Mouth and Throat: There is mild erythema around and underneath the nares.
Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. There are a few erythematous papules on the bilateral medial cheeks.

ASSESSMENT

• Perioral dermatitis.
• Rosacea.
• Ocular rosacea.

PLAN

I recommended that she continue to use the sulfacetamide wash once a day. She can continue to use the metro-nidazole cream. I recommended that she increase the doxycycline to twice a day. She can discontinue the use of the Elidel cream. I recommended that she use it until the rash is gone, and then she can stop it and use it again if the rash starts to flair again. She can hold off until she sees me again before she starts any old products. She will follow up with me in 4 weeks.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue to use the sulfacetamide wash once a day. Continue to use the metro-nidazole cream. She can discontinue the use of the Elidel cream. She can use it until the rash is gone, and then she can stop it and use it again if the rash starts to flair again. She can hold off until she sees me again before she starts any old products. She will follow up with me in 4 weeks.

PROCEDURES

A prescription for tacrolimus ointment was written.

INSTRUCTIONS

Apply to rash on face twice daily until rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. Once the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. She can continue doxycycline another week and then stop. She can continue to use the face wash and metro-nidazole cream.

The patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks.

ADDITIONAL PHARMACY INSTRUCTIONS

Doxycycline: She has enough refills.

Metro-nidazole cream: She has enough.

Elidel cream: Cancel.

Vanicream: Given to the patient for samples.

Petroleum jelly: Given to the patient for samples.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
