Constitutional: Denies fevers.
Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea and vomiting.
Hematologic: Denies hematochezia, melena, or hematemesis.
Neurological: Denies weakness or numbness.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

Respiratory
- Respiratory rate: 16
- Oxygen saturation on room air: 99%

Cardiovascular
- Heart rate: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No clicks or murmurs.

Gastrointestinal
- Examination of the abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Slight guarding present to the right upper quadrant. Murphy's sign present. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the wall is noted. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been experiencing right upper quadrant pain for several weeks. He has been trying to modify his diet with minimal improvement. He has a family history of cholecystitis.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. He is not interested in dietary modifications, so I recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I explained the procedure and postoperative expectations, including avoiding high fat foods and high fiber diet.
