Follow-up after interior stemi.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julia Smith is a 63-year-old female who presents for a follow-up after an interior stemi, which occurred 2 months ago. She had an RCA stent placed at the time. The patient reports that she was doing well until she started experiencing chest pain that would not go away. She went to the emergency room and was told she needed another stent. The patient denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily for about a mile and a half to 2 miles. She denies any leg swelling. She has noticed that her legs are a little swollen since she was discharged from the hospital. She denies lying flat at night, but states she may sit up a bit more. She states she is able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She is watching her salt intake, but is not strict about it. She states that she has been watching her sugar levels and is careful not to go on insulin.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that she enjoys walking her dog. She loves pizza and tries. She states she is not watching her salt intake as strictly as she should be.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.
• Respiratory: Denies dyspnea.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies leg swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Neck
	+ General Examination: No jugular venous distention.
• Cardiovascular
	+ Auscultation of the heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.
