Hospital follow-up.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is an 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.

The patient states that he has been trying to watch his diet as instructed. He notes that he has noticed that his ankles have been swelling and that one day he became very short of breath and even had to call 911. The patient states that he was placed on oxygen in the ambulance when he arrived at the emergency room. He was diagnosed with heart failure and was discharged home on Bumex 2 mg daily. The patient states that he has been watching his diet, but admits that he has been having some slips. He reports that he is a big cheese lover and enjoys going to parties on the weekends. He also enjoys pizza and chicken wings. He denies chest pain.

The patient states that he was in the hospital for a couple of days and was given a medication that made him go to the bathroom frequently and an IV water pill. He reports that he was also started on new medications for his blood pressure. He denies shortness of breath or difficulty sleeping. He reports that he is able to get up and move around and has no problem sleeping. He also denies any chest pain.

The patient states that he has been checking his blood pressure at home with a blood pressure cuff that he purchased. He reports that his blood pressure has been good.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports lower extremity edema.

VITALS

• Blood pressure: 128/72 mmHg.
• Respiratory rate: 26 breaths per minute.
• Heart rate: 79 beats per minute.
• Oxygen saturation: 99%.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Neck
	+ General Examination: No jugular venous distention.
• Respiratory
	+ Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular
	+ Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.
• Musculoskeletal
	+ Examination: Trace lower extremity edema.

RESULTS

Echocardiogram reveals a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Congestive heart failure.
	* Medical Reasoning: This appears to be secondary to dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.
	* Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of adhering to a low salt diet. He should continue to watch his diet and avoid salty foods. He should also continue to take Bumex 2 mg daily. He should weigh himself daily and contact me if he gains 3 lbs in 2 days.
	* Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily.
	* Additional Testing: Referral to a nutritionist for education on diet.

2. Hypertension.
