Right ankle pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Davis is a pleasant 55-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his symptoms began yesterday after he slipped on the ice. The patient was going out to take the trash when he slipped and fell on the ice. He was with a friend who was visiting and she helped him up. The patient was able to elevate and ice his right ankle last night and today he is able to bear some weight on it but he is still limping. The patient reports that he is feeling a little better today and that ibuprofen has been helpful.

The patient denies any numbness in the right ankle.

The patient reports that he has never injured this ankle before.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he plays a lot of soccer and is currently playing in a community league.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that his husband is also very into soccer.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he has been taking ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds in the right foot. Strong dorsalis pedis pulse.
NEURO: Sensation is intact to light touch in the right ankle.
MSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right ankle was obtained and reviewed in office today. It revealed no fracture.

ASSESSMENT

Right ankle sprain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that he keep his leg elevated, especially when seated, and to continue to ice his right ankle. I have also recommended that he be placed into an aircast and provided crutches for the next 1 to 2 days. He may begin weight-bearing as tolerated after that time. I have also recommended that he take NSAIDs as needed for pain. We will follow up in 2 weeks to see how he is doing.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. If he experiences any symptoms in the meantime, he is encouraged to contact the clinic. }}<|eot_id|>
